WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.067
A rolling deployment replaces workers in stages
so the service can remain available. First

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:10.078
stop assigning new requests to one worker, then
allow its existing work to drain within a

00:00:10.078 --> 00:00:15.089
defined deadline. Deploy the new version, verify
that it is ready to serve, and only then

00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:18.580
restore it to the active pool before moving to
another worker.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:23.026
The illustration separates draining from
readiness because removing a worker from new

00:00:23.026 --> 00:00:27.786
assignments does not instantly end its in-flight
work. This procedure needs spare capacity:

00:00:27.786 --> 00:00:32.441
if the remaining pool cannot handle the
workload, a technically orderly rollout can
still

00:00:32.441 --> 00:00:36.259
create excessive queueing and errors for users
throughout the deployment.

00:00:36.259 --> 00:00:41.497
Health checks provide observations that help the
balancer decide where to send new work. A

00:00:41.497 --> 00:00:46.851
failed probe might indicate a crashed process,
but it might also reflect overload, a network

00:00:46.851 --> 00:00:48.713
problem, or a failed dependency.

00:00:48.759 --> 00:00:54.033
The diagram shows a healthy response maintaining
membership and repeated failed observations

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:59.249
leading to removal. Thresholds avoid reacting to
a single transient failure, at the cost of

00:00:59.249 --> 00:01:04.236
slower detection. During that interval, some
real requests may still reach the affected

00:01:04.236 --> 00:01:04.637
worker.

00:01:04.759 --> 00:01:10.018
Explain how the client handles those failures
and whether a retry is safe. Removing a worker

00:01:10.018 --> 00:01:14.763
changes future routing; it does not prove that
previous writes did not take effect.
